8. Relationship Between PCB Layout and Flight-Control Performance

The PCB is a hardware platform for the FPV flight-control and navigation system. Its layout can affect the reliability of sensor measurements, power distribution, communication, and debugging.

8.1 Signal integrity

The controller communicates with peripherals through electrical interfaces. Poor routing, unsuitable return paths, or excessive noise can interfere with these communications.

Careful track routing, suitable grounding, and appropriate component placement help improve signal integrity.

8.2 Power integrity

The controller and sensors require stable supply voltages.

Poor power distribution or inadequate decoupling can cause voltage fluctuations, unexpected resets, or unreliable peripheral operation.

The power-distribution network should therefore be checked for trace resistance, current capacity, capacitor placement, and regulator stability.

8.3 Noise management

Switching regulators and other noisy circuits can affect sensitive sensor measurements if the PCB is not designed carefully.

Good layout practices include minimizing switching-current loops, maintaining suitable ground references, and keeping sensitive sensor signals away from noisy nodes where practical.

8.4 Mechanical reliability

The PCB must be securely mounted and protected from mechanical interference.

Mounting-hole positions, connector access, component height, and clearance from the UAV frame should be verified before manufacturing.

These design considerations help create a more reliable hardware platform, but actual flight performance must be established through testing.

9. PCB Design Verification Before Fabrication

Before sending the PCB for fabrication, the design should be checked using the PCB design software and the intended manufacturer's requirements.

Electrical checks
Run the Electrical Rules Check (ERC) on the schematic.
Run the Design Rule Check (DRC) on the PCB.
Verify all intended net connections.
Check for unintended shorts and unconnected pads.
Confirm power-net assignments.
Verify connector pinouts and polarity.
Check component footprints against their datasheets.
Layout checks
Confirm board dimensions and mounting-hole positions.
Verify copper clearances.
Check trace widths for the expected current.
Check via sizes and spacing.
Review ground-plane continuity.
Inspect power-plane connections.
Verify copper-to-board-edge clearances.
Inspect sensitive signal routes and switching-regulator layouts.
Manufacturing checks
Verify the final layer stackup with the PCB manufacturer.
Check solder-mask and silkscreen settings.
Confirm drill files and plated-hole requirements.
Generate and inspect the required manufacturing outputs, such as Gerber and drill files.
Review the 3D model for mechanical compatibility.

Passing these checks reduces the risk of fabrication and assembly problems. It does not, by itself, guarantee that the assembled PCB will operate correctly.

10. Testing and Validation Plan

After fabrication and assembly, the board should be tested progressively.

Step 1 – Visual inspection

Inspect the PCB for damaged components, solder bridges, incorrect component orientation, and visible manufacturing defects.

Step 2 – Power-off continuity checks

Check for unintended shorts between power rails and ground before applying power.

Step 3 – Initial power-up

Apply power using an appropriate current-limited supply. Measure the input voltage and each required output rail.

Step 4 – Controller verification

Confirm that the controller receives the correct supply voltage and that its programming or debugging interface works as intended.

Step 5 – Peripheral verification

Test the motion sensors and other peripherals individually. Verify that the controller can communicate with each device and obtain valid data.

Step 6 – Interface verification

Check the receiver, navigation, telemetry, and ESC interfaces against the schematic and their respective electrical specifications.

Step 7 – System-level testing

After individual interfaces have been verified, test the integrated system under controlled conditions.

Safety note: Do not connect propellers during initial flight-controller testing. Verify the power system, firmware, sensor readings, and ESC outputs before performing any controlled flight test.

11. Conclusion

The PCB layout and 3D model provide complementary views of the THEJAS32-based FPV Flight Control and Navigation System.

The copper-layer views show how electrical connections and power distribution are organized across the PCB. The 3D models show the physical arrangement of components and connectors on both sides of the board.

Together, these views support the review of electrical routing, component placement, mechanical fit, and manufacturing readiness.

The final design should be validated through schematic checks, PCB design-rule checks, manufacturing-file inspection, electrical testing, and controlled system-level verification before being used in an FPV UAV.

